This page follows one byte of application traffic through transport v2, one
layer at a time, and names the file or contract that owns each exact value.
[YUME explained](EXPLAINED.md) has the overall picture and the figures, and the
[glossary](GLOSSARY.md) defines the terms.

## Substance and disguise

@table
| Order on the way out | Layer | Kind | Why it exists |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | AES-256-GCM seal | Substance | Keeps the payload secret and detects tampering, under a key used once |
| 2 | YUME frame header | Substance | Marks where a message ends and which stream it belongs to |
| 3 | WebSocket binary message | Disguise | Web applications keep live two-way channels open with WebSocket |
| 4 | HTTP/2 DATA | Disguise | Browsers speak HTTP/2 to websites |
| 5 | TLS 1.3 | Both | Encrypts the carrier and authenticates the server, with a handshake shaped like the captured browser |
| Last | TCP to the HTTPS port | Network | Carries the connection |
@end

A change to layer 1 or 2 is a security change. Layers 3 and 4 add no
protection, so a change there leaves the seal intact but can remove the
disguise, which only a comparison against a capture reveals. In TLS, the
encryption and the exporter that AUTH binds to are protection, and the
handshake shape is disguise.

## Reading bytes

A byte is 8 bits, written as two hexadecimal digits. Each digit is 4 bits,
called a nibble. In `0x7C` the high nibble is `7` and the low nibble is `C`.

AND keeps a bit only where both sides have it, so it clears parts of a value.
OR sets a bit where either side has it, so it writes parts of a value.

@table
| Step | Hex | Bits | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Start | `0x7C` | `0111 1100` | Some value |
| Mask | `0xF0` | `1111 0000` | Keep the high nibble |
| AND result | `0x70` | `0111 0000` | The low nibble is cleared |
| OR with | `0x0A` | `0000 1010` | Write `A` into the low nibble |
| Final | `0x7A` | `0111 1010` | High nibble kept, low nibble forced |
@end

The TLS GREASE values of RFC 8701 follow that pattern: each byte has `A` as its
low nibble.

Big-endian means the most significant byte comes first, the way people write
numbers, and YUME's wire formats use it. A length prefix means a message starts
by stating its own size, so the reader knows where it ends. The frame header
relies on one.

## Layer by layer

### Entry

Traffic reaches YUME through a SOCKS5 proxy, in which the application names the
host and port before sending data, a port forward, packet routing through a TUN
device, or the C ABI when a program embeds YUME. The client learns where the
application wants to go, and that destination travels inside the tunnel.

Owners: `src/client/proxy/socks.cpp`, `src/client/packet/`, `src/abi/`.

### 1. The seal

The seal makes the payload unreadable, makes any change detectable, and stops a
captured message from being replayed or moved. AES-256-GCM is an AEAD,
authenticated encryption with associated data:

- Decryption checks a tag computed over the ciphertext. If one bit changed, it
  fails instead of returning garbage.
- Reusing a nonce under the same GCM key breaks both secrecy and authenticity.
  YUME derives a fresh key for every message, erases it after use, and builds
  the nonce from the direction and the sequence number.
- Associated data is authenticated but not encrypted. YUME includes the
  protocol label, the profile, the direction, the epoch, the sequence, and the
  frame's type, stream, and flags, so a captured message cannot be replayed,
  sent back in the other direction, or moved to another stream or position.

Neither side sends a key. Both take part in X25519 and ML-KEM-1024 exchanges,
add the pre-shared file secret and the TLS exporter, and derive the same root
key, as the key figure in [YUME explained](EXPLAINED.md) shows.

Owners: `src/core/security/session_ratchet.cpp`, `src/core/security/ratchet.cpp`,
`src/core/security/auth_v2.cpp`, `src/core/security/channel_binding.cpp`, and
the key schedule in the [wire contract](protocol/YUME_2_0_WIRE.md).

### 2. The frame header

One tunnel carries many connections as one run of bytes, so each message needs
a boundary and a stream. An 8-byte header in front of every sealed message
carries a length, a type, a stream number, and flags. The reader consumes
exactly that many bytes, and the next header follows. The type separates
application data from control, authentication, and rekey messages.

The peer controls the declared length. A reader that allocates before
validating it gives the peer a way to exhaust memory. YUME's readers refuse an
oversized declaration at the header, before allocating anything, and apply a
smaller budget before authentication.

The transport-v2 stream number is one byte, which caps streams per connection.
YTP/1 widens it to 31 bits, and that needs a new wire format.

Owners: `src/core/protocol/protocol.hpp` for the header, types, flags, and
payload cap, and `src/core/protocol/frame_limits.hpp` for pre-authentication
budgets.

### 3. WebSocket

Each framed message becomes a WebSocket binary message, the way a web
application sends data over a live channel. WebSocket requires clients to mask
their frames and servers not to. Masking adds no security, and an unmasked
client frame would violate the protocol. The codec also accepts fragmented
messages and interleaved control frames.

Owners: `src/stealth/websocket_codec.hpp`, `src/stealth/h2_carrier.cpp`.

### 4. HTTP/2

HTTP/2 is binary and multiplexed. One connection carries many numbered streams,
and everything is cut into typed frames such as HEADERS, DATA, SETTINGS, and
WINDOW_UPDATE. TLS negotiates it during the handshake. HTTP/2 streams and frames
are unrelated to YUME's own streams and frames, so a claim about a stream limit
or credit has to say which one it means.

The client loads the cover page and its assets on ordinary streams, then opens
one more stream with an extended CONNECT, the standard way to run WebSocket over
HTTP/2. Tunnel traffic rides as DATA frames on that stream, and the connection
stays valid HTTP/2 throughout.

HTTP/2 flow control makes a receiver grant credit, and a sender may transmit
only as many bytes as it has been granted. YUME holds a tunnel byte's credit
until the destination consumes it. A queue overrun is fixed with admission and
backpressure. A larger queue only delays the failure.

Owners: `src/stealth/h2_carrier.cpp`, the captured settings in
`tests/fixtures/chrome151-node24/`, and [stealth transport](STEALTH.md).

### 5. TLS 1.3 and the ClientHello

TLS encrypts everything above it but sends its first message, the ClientHello,
in the clear. The list of ciphers, extensions, groups, and signature algorithms
in that message, in its exact order, identifies the software that sent it.
Hashes of that structure, such as JA3 and JA4, fingerprint clients without
decrypting anything.

RFC 8701 GREASE has clients send reserved values that servers must ignore, so
middleboxes keep tolerating unknown extensions. Browsers vary those values, and
a fixed or missing pattern is itself a fingerprint.

Stock OpenSSL cannot express every row of the captured browser's ClientHello.
YUME's pinned OpenSSL build carries an opt-in capability that reproduces that
structure, selected by an immutable transport profile.

TLS 1.3 authenticates the server with a signature over the handshake
transcript, and both sides derive the session key by key agreement. YUME's own
identities also sign, with two algorithms at once.

Owners: `patches/`, `src/stealth/tls_client_profile.cpp` for GREASE
selection, [transport profiles](TRANSPORT_PROFILES.md), and
[stealth transport](STEALTH.md) for what may be claimed.

### The return path

The layers unwrap in reverse. The server opens the destination socket, so the
destination sees the server's address, and the server sees the authenticated
identity and the destination. The [threat model](THREAT_MODEL.md) owns that
trust boundary.

## Why callbacks cause crashes

Code starts a read and hands over a callback to run when bytes arrive. The
function that started the read returns at once, and an event loop runs the
callback when the operation finishes.

A session starts a read with a callback that needs the session. The connection
drops and the session is destroyed while the read is pending. The read
completes, the callback runs, and it reaches for memory that has been freed.
That use-after-free depends on timing, can corrupt memory without crashing, and
a peer that drops connections at chosen moments may be able to trigger it.

Two rules follow:

- Callbacks hold weak references. A weak reference does not keep the session
  alive but can be checked, so a callback whose session is gone does nothing. A
  strong reference causes the opposite bug: the pending work keeps the session
  alive forever.
- Teardown neither allocates nor calls callbacks. An allocation can fail and
  leave cleanup half done, and a callback can re-enter the object being
  destroyed. Handlers are detached under the lock and destroyed after it is
  released, and tests force allocation failure to check that cleanup survives.

For any asynchronous change, ask: if this connection dies while that operation
is in flight, what runs when it completes, and what does it touch?

## Races

Adding one to a counter is three steps: read, add, write. Two threads can both
read 5 and both write 6, and one increment disappears without an error. With a
pointer or a queue length, the same interleaving corrupts memory or frees
something twice.

The window is a few instructions wide, so races appear under load, on more
cores, or with another compiler, and a log line can hide them. ThreadSanitizer
finds them by recording which thread touched what, even on runs that came out
right. Locks add deadlocks: two paths that take the same locks in different
orders can each wait for the other forever. That is why lock order is fixed and
why code avoids calling a callback while holding a lock.

## Reviewing a change by layer

These questions expose most shallow work without reading C++:

- Did the change touch substance or disguise? Framing and crypto changes are
  security changes. WebSocket, HTTP/2, and ClientHello changes are validated
  only against a capture.
- If this connection dies while that operation is in flight, what runs, and
  what does it touch?
- This length comes from the peer. Where is it checked, and does the check come
  before any allocation?
- Is that stream limit on YUME's wire or on HTTP/2's?
- Does this change what a capture would show, and which capture was it compared
  against?
- A queue overrun was fixed by raising the cap. What made the queue fill?
- Which file owns this value, and is it stated anywhere else?

When a comment and a test disagree, the test wins. A value stated in two files
drifts, and no build notices, so each fact has one owner. The
[source map](SOURCE_MAP.md) and the reading table in
[YUME explained](EXPLAINED.md) point at the owners.
